\begin{proof}
Suppose, toward a contradiction, that \(\bigcup_{n\ge 1}A_n=B\cup C\), where \(B\) and \(C\) are disjoint nonempty open subsets of \(\bigcup_{n\ge 1}A_n\). Since \(A_1\) is connected, it lies entirely in either \(B\) or \(C\). Without loss of generality, let \(A_1\subset B\).

Assume \(A_n\subset B\). Since \(A_{n+1}\) is connected and is contained in \(B\cup C\), it lies entirely in \(B\) or entirely in \(C\). It cannot lie in \(C\), because then \(A_n\cap A_{n+1}\subset B\cap C=\varnothing\), contradicting \(A_n\cap A_{n+1}\ne\varnothing\). Hence \(A_{n+1}\subset B\). By induction, \(A_n\subset B\) for every \(n\), so \(\bigcup_{n\ge1}A_n\subset B\), contradicting that \(C\) is nonempty. Therefore \(\bigcup_{n\ge1}A_n\) is connected.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let \(U=\bigcup_{n\ge1}A_n\). Suppose that \(U\) is disconnected. Then \(U=B\cup C\), where \(B,C\) are disjoint nonempty open subsets of \(U\). Because \(A_1\) is connected and \(A_1\subset U=B\cup C\), we have \(A_1\subset B\) or \(A_1\subset C\). Relabeling \(B\) and \(C\) if necessary, assume \(A_1\subset B\).

We claim inductively that \(A_n\subset B\) for every \(n\). This is true for \(n=1\). If \(A_n\subset B\), then connectedness of \(A_{n+1}\) implies that \(A_{n+1}\subset B\) or \(A_{n+1}\subset C\). The latter is impossible, since \(A_n\cap A_{n+1}\ne\varnothing\) would give a point in \(B\cap C\). Thus \(A_{n+1}\subset B\). Consequently, \(U\subset B\), contradicting \(C\ne\varnothing\). Hence \(U\) is connected.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Set \(U=\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}A_n\). Assume that \(U\) admits a separation \(U=V\cup W\), with \(V\) and \(W\) disjoint, nonempty, open subsets of \(U\). Since \(A_1\) is connected, it is contained in one side of the separation. Interchanging the sides if necessary, suppose \(A_1\subset V\).

For each \(n\), we show that \(A_n\subset V\). Suppose \(A_n\subset V\). The connected set \(A_{n+1}\subset V\cup W\) must be contained wholly in \(V\) or wholly in \(W\). If it were contained in \(W\), then the assumed nonempty intersection \(A_n\cap A_{n+1}\) would be contained in \(V\cap W\), which is empty. Therefore \(A_{n+1}\subset V\). Induction gives \(A_n\subset V\) for all \(n\), and hence \(U\subset V\), contradicting \(W\ne\varnothing\). Thus no separation exists, and \(U\) is connected.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let \(U=\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}A_n\). To prove that \(U\) is connected, suppose for contradiction that \(U=V\cup W\), where \(V\) and \(W\) are disjoint nonempty open subsets of \(U\).

The connected set \(A_1\) is contained in \(V\cup W\), so it must be contained in one of the two sets. After exchanging \(V\) and \(W\) if necessary, assume \(A_1\subset V\). We now prove by induction that \(A_n\subset V\) for every \(n\). The assertion holds for \(n=1\). If \(A_n\subset V\), then the connected set \(A_{n+1}\subset V\cup W\) is contained in either \(V\) or \(W\). It cannot be contained in \(W\), because \(A_n\cap A_{n+1}\ne\varnothing\) would then imply \(A_n\cap A_{n+1}\subset V\cap W=\varnothing\). Hence \(A_{n+1}\subset V\).

Thus every \(A_n\) lies in \(V\), so \(U\subset V\), contradicting the nonemptiness of \(W\). Therefore \(U\) has no separation and is connected.
\end{proof}
